Kernir patch 0022: the seekable zip reader holds its central directory to the entry and names limits, and a zip64
end record's central directory offset to the bytes before it

Upstream: libarchive 3.8.10, libarchive/archive_read_support_format_zip.c (read_zip64_eocd,
slurp_central_directory), include/kernir_names.h (its comment). Applies on top of 0001-0020 (0012, 0016 and 0020
lines included).

The seekable reader reads the whole central directory before the first header: it callocs a zip_entry (about 190
bytes) for every 46-byte record and parses every record's extra fields, records whose local header offset repeats
included, which the offset tree drops and the reader never returns. A 23 MB zip of 500,000 such records held 120 MB
before its first header, whatever Kernir's entry limit; the claimed entry count and (zip64) size are not used. Each
record's Info-ZIP Unicode Path field (0x7075) was also parsed against the archive_entry, whose pathname nothing in
the central directory sets, so one record's 65,000-byte Unicode path stayed there and every later 56-byte record
with a 0x7075 field whose CRC did not match hashed it again: an 11 MB zip of 200,000 such records cost 5.3 s of CPU
before its first header. read_zip64_eocd took any central directory offset.

The records are now held to 0016's limits: the ones with a new local header offset (the headers returned, which
Kernir counts) to max_entries, every record to 2 × max_entries (saturated at UINT64_MAX) before it is allocated, and
the record names to max(names_bytes, name_bytes_per_entry × the listed records so far)
(__archive_read_kernir_names_allowance); ARCHIVE_FATAL, ARCHIVE_ERRNO_FILE_FORMAT, own messages ("ZIP archive has
more entries than the entry limit", "ZIP entry names take more than the names limit"), which Kernir maps to its entry
and names limits. With mac-ext (Kernir turns it off) a resource fork record is not listed and expose_parent_dirs'
moves are not counted; the 2 × bound still holds. The names charged are the records' (the local headers' may
differ), in central directory order, against the allowance of the records listed so far and without link targets,
where Kernir charges the headers' names, link targets included, in local header offset order: a crafted central
directory whose long names come before the records that grow the allowance can be refused though Kernir would take
its headers (names within names_bytes never are; nor is one in offset order, with no repeated offset and the local
headers' names, unless Kernir refuses its headers too). slurp_central_directory passes no archive_entry to
process_extra: the central directory's 0x7075 names were never used (zip_read_local_file_header sets the pathname
from the local header and its own fields). A zip64 end record whose central directory offset is past the record's own
offset gets no seekable bid from it (the streaming reader then reads the local headers); its size is not checked, as
nothing reads it (slurp_central_directory starts at the offset and stops at an end record), so a wrong size keeps the
seekable reader and the central directory's data. Archives within the limits read as before.

Apply from Sources/CLibArchive: patch -p1 < patches/0022-zip-central-directory-limits.patch

